lens_summary:
GitHub intel, read 25 Sep 2026 10:40-10:55 UTC (log: research/wf_ideas_v3/h_github-intel.notes.md). I searched about 140 competition repos with the GitHub MCP (search_repositories and search_code across all of GitHub) and read the useful ones through raw.githubusercontent.com. api.github.com, github.com HTML, codeload and public.czbiohub.org all return 403 here.

Main finding: no public GitHub repo reports a legitimate public LB above 0.947. The best are:
- alvaromendizabal 0.947, a Harmonic Fusion reproduction;
- naveenlx111-svg 0.947 anchor (its 0.946 submissions were linker changes);
- JunhaoLiXD 0.941/0.944;
- Kai-Gowers 0.942 reference and 0.927 with their own weights;
- dalloliogm 0.939-0.946;
- matheuspf, local only (0.935 in-sample on 199 clips);
- sota1111 0.626.
The 0.955 in KHHH2312/vibecode is a July score from the division metric hack that was patched on 17 Jul.

x138 (0.953) exists only as a Kaggle notebook. Its building-block authors are now above it privately: Teddy Tennant 0.959, Anvith Pothula 0.956. Neither has a biohub repo: search_code with user:teddytennant (209 repos) and user:anvithpothula returned 0 relevant hits. The 0.969-0.975 teams have published nothing. So there is no technique above 0.953 on GitHub to reproduce.

What GitHub does give, all consistent with our closed lines:
(1) Retraining the public recipe from scratch on 179 videos (4-GPU DDP, 400 epochs) scored 0.927 on the LB against 0.942 for the shipped weights. Their local held-out gap was detection recall (96.7 vs 98.3%). Epoch 150 was as good as epoch 385. (Kai-Gowers)
(2) A linker/post-processing change with a large in-sample gain died on the LB: 'preserve strong learned edges before relink' went 0.943 -> 0.971 on 8 in-sample movies and scored 0.946 public against a 0.947 anchor. The HOCT consensus veto also scored 0.946. (naveenlx111-svg)
(3) matheuspf's 199-clip error anatomy:
- 80% of missed divisions have both the parent and the daughters matched, but no fork was predicted;
- 71% of unmatched annotations have a raw detector peak within 7 um but no final node.
(4) In the public stack, motion relink plus the 14 um cap undo correct transformer links on frames with whole-field jumps (Kai-Gowers).
(5) The official royerlab training recipe and the Ultrack sparse-zebrafish configuration. That configuration covers the dorado movie (521 timepoints) and uses unet-simview.pt maps. The team had already measured that Ultrack points are not a position oracle (GT median 1.29 um from them).

Two concrete, cheap gaps came from reading the x138 code itself (experiments/ext_x138/biohub-x138.ipynb):
- the readmitted and gap-filled peaks never pass through the V1284 head; they stay on the 1.625 um coarse grid;
- the head is applied only once.

Honest ceiling of this lens: it adds no step change. The four ideas below are worth about +0.001 to +0.003 in expectation, most of it below LB resolution (noise about 0.003). 0.99 is not reachable: it would beat the public #1 (0.975) by 0.015, with no public evidence of any method above 0.975. The best reachable target is to lock in x138 at about 0.953, plus DaughterNet insertion, plus these riders, for about 0.954-0.957. Anything above that needs a new detector and linker trained on dense or extra labels, which is weeks of work and not doable by 29 Sep.

hypotheses.validation_protocol:
No offline validation is possible or needed for a verbatim copy: the author's public score is the pre-registered expectation.
Gate:
- (a) the dummy-test run completes with no REPAIR FAILED;
- (b) node/edge counts on the 4 dummy clips are within 1% of the author's logs, if shown;
- (c) on the LB, adopt it as F1 only if its score >= max(#34, #35) + 0.002 (noise 0.003, so a tie does not replace the verified x138 base).
Never mix it with our riders before the verbatim copy has scored.

Offline, before any slot. Friend A (5070 Ti) or a Kaggle GPU runs a modified capture (mode 'capture2') on 40 train videos, 20 per embryo, none of them among the 10 probable x138 training videos. It stores, per frame, the integer peaks, the 224-d features at the integer peaks, and the 224-d TRILINEAR features at the pass-1-refined positions.
CPU evaluation with tools/train_coordhead.py-style metrics (fixed GT pairs <= 4 um) for {none, pass1, pass2 damp 0.5, pass2 damp 1.0}.
Pre-registered gate, all required:
- (a) pass2 mean distance <= 0.97 x pass1 on EACH embryo separately;
- (b) 7 um re-match count >= pass1 and share <= 4 um >= pass1 on each embryo;
- (c) the all-peak mean shift of pass 2 relative to pass 1 is <= 0.15 um per axis (it must not learn a convention);
- (d) on the dummy clips, node and edge counts are within 1% of exp56.
Then one LB slot: x138 + cascade. Adopt it only if public >= #34 (tie allowed, since it is out-of-sample-validated). Kill it if the drop is >= 0.002.

Offline gate on the user's PC (the captures exist; the CPU run takes about 5 min), with the tools/train_coordhead.py kill-test harness plus a blend arm. Pre-registered, all required:
- (a) embryo-held-out, both directions: blend(public, Q trained on the other embryo) mean distance <= public x 1.00 (a no-worse gate, because the public comparator is in-embryo in both directions: the asymmetry correction);
- (b) leak5 OOF on each embryo separately: blend <= 0.95 x public;
- (c) all-peak mean shift of the blend within 0.15 um of the public head per axis;
- (d) 7 um re-match count >= public in every fold.
Only then: private head dataset, x138 + blend kernel, dummy run, one LB slot. Adopt it only if public >= #34 score. It enters finals only as F1' (not F2, to keep the finals structurally diverse).
